      "Monitor vital signs for changes (e.g., orthostatic hypoten-sion, tachycardia, or fe v er) that may cause or be the effect of dehydration.",
      "Weigh the client and compare with recent weight history. Perform serial weights to determine trends.",
      "Review laboratory data (e.g., hemoglobin [Hb]/hematocrit [Hct], osmolality , electrolytes [e.g., sodium and potassium], blood urea nitrogen [B UN]/creatinine [Cr]) as indicated to evaluate fl uid and electrolyte status.",
      "Offer a variety of fl uids and w ater-rich foods, and make them available throughout the day, if the client is able to take oral fl uids. Assist/remind the client to drink, as needed. Determine individual fl uid needs and establish replacement over 24 hr to increase the client’s daily fl uid intake.",
      "Administer medications as appropriate (e.g., antiemetics, antidiarrheals, or antip yretics) to stop or limit fl uid losses.",
      "Provide nutritionally balanced diet and/or enteral feedings, when indicated (a v oiding use of hyperosmolar or excessively high-protein formulas), and provide an adequate amount of free water with feedings.",
