      "Note cultural values or religious beliefs and confl icts that may e xist. Individuals are enculturated as they grow up and, depending on particular family views and taboos, may har-bor feelings of shame and guilt about their sexual feelings.",
      "Assess stress factors in client’s environment that might cause anxiety or psychological reactions (e.g., po wer issues in volving SO, adult children, aging, employment, loss of prowess).",
      "Explore knowledge of effects of altered body function/limi-tations precipitated by illness (e.g., MS, arthritis, mutilating cancer sur gery) or medical treatment of alternati ve sexual responses and expressions (e.g., undescended testicle in young male, gender change or reassignment procedure).",
      "Review history of substance use (prescription medications, ov er-the-counter drugs, alcohol, illicit drugs). May be used by client to handle underlying feelings or anxiety.",
      "Explore issues and fears associated with sex (pregnancy, sexually transmitted, trust and control issues, infl exible beliefs, preference confusion, lack of privacy, altered performance).",
      "Determine client’s interpretation of the altered sexual activ-ity or beha vior (e.g., a w ay of controlling, relief of anxiety, pleasure, lack of partner). These behaviors (when related to body changes, including pregnancy, weight loss or gain, or loss of body part) may refl ect a stage of grieving. Nursing Priority No. 2. To assist client/SO to deal with individual situation:",
      "Provide atmosphere in which discussion of sexual problems is encouraged and permitted. Sense of trust or comf ort enhances ability to discuss sensiti ve matters.",
      "Avoid value judgments— they do not help the client cope with the situation.",
